# TCP handshake vectors: what this stack must put on the wire while a
# connection is being opened, and what it must put there when one is refused.
#
# These lines are the SPECIFICATION. Where the implementation disagrees with a
# line here, the implementation is what changes. A line is only edited if it
# can be shown to be wrong, and then the reason is written down next to it.
#
# ## Scenarios
#
# Every TCP line in this format carries the SAME four-tuple: `VectorFrames`
# pins the ports at 50000 -> 8080 and the addresses at 192.168.127.2 ->
# 192.168.127.1, because a packetdrill-style script describes one connection
# already in progress and the port pair is never the thing under test. So a
# single script cannot hold both "a listener answers" and "nothing is
# listening": those are statements about the same tuple. The file is therefore
# divided into scenarios by a `# scenario: <name>` marker, each replayed
# against its own freshly built stack. `TCPHandshakeVectorTests` cross-checks
# the set of markers here against the set of tests that run them, so a
# scenario added below and forgotten there fails rather than sits unexecuted.
#
# ## Sequence numbers are ABSOLUTE, not packetdrill's relative form
#
# `VectorRunner` compares decoded wire numbers, so every number here is the
# number on the wire. Our ISS is 1000 and the guest's is 0 unless a line says
# otherwise. The plan drafted these vectors with our ISS at 0 as well; it is
# 1000 here so that OUR sequence space and the GUEST's cannot be confused for
# one another. With both at 0 the SYN-ACK's `ack 1` is simultaneously IRS+1 and
# ISS+1, and a stack that acknowledged the wrong one of those would match the
# vector exactly. That is the only reason the numbers differ from the plan's.
#
# ## `win` is stated on every expected line, including the resets
#
# `VectorFrames.decode` always reports the window field, so an expected line
# that omits `win` is not "don't care" -- it is a claim that the field is
# absent, which no TCP segment can satisfy. A reset carries `win 0`, and
# saying so is what makes that a fact this file pins rather than one it leaves
# to whatever the code happens to do.


# scenario: passive-open
#
# The guest connects; the gateway accepts. The guest offers a window scale and
# SACK; we answer with our own scale and accept SACK.
#
# `wscale 3` rather than the 7 the guest offered: the scale is not a value both
# sides agree on, it is each side's own, and ours is derived from what the
# reassembler can actually hold. Advertising `wscale 7` beside `win 65535`
# would promise the peer 8 MB against a 256 KiB buffer -- it fills the pipe it
# was promised, we drop most of it, and the connection presents as a lossy path
# with no error raised anywhere.
#
# `sackOK` was once omitted here for a related reason, and the comment said so:
# RFC 2018 expects a receiver that sent it to send SACK blocks back, and this
# stack discarded them. It does not any more, so the option is now answered.
# The two cases only looked alike -- the scale's problem was a promise about
# capacity, which is still real; SACK's was an unimplemented feature, which is
# the kind of reason that expires.
0.000 < S  0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460,wscale 7,sackOK>
0.000 > S. 1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460,wscale 3,sackOK>
# The handshake's third leg. It acknowledges our SYN and occupies no sequence
# space, so it draws nothing back: two peers that acknowledged each other's
# acknowledgements would never stop. The runner reports any frame this file
# does not account for, so "nothing" here is checked, not assumed.
0.010 < .  1:1(0) ack 1001 win 512


# scenario: closed-port
#
# A SYN for a port nothing is listening on is REFUSED, not ignored. A guest
# that hangs until its own connect() timeout is a far worse failure than one
# told immediately that the door is shut.
#
# The refusal is `R.` -- a reset with the ACK bit SET. RFC 9293 section
# 3.10.7.1 requires <SEQ=0><ACK=SEG.SEQ+SEG.LEN><CTL=RST,ACK> for an incoming
# segment that carries no ACK of its own, and SEG.LEN counts the SYN, so a bare
# SYN at sequence 0 is acknowledged with 1. A bare RST -- or one acknowledging
# 0 -- is discarded by the peer as unverifiable, which the guest cannot tell
# apart from the port never answering at all. This is the branch of that rule
# taken when the arriving segment has NO ACK; `stray-ack` below takes the other.
0.100 < S  0:0(0) win 65535
0.100 > R. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 0


# scenario: stray-ack
#
# An ACK for a connection that is in no state at all -- no SYN preceded it.
# There IS a listener on this port, so this is the endpoint's own refusal path
# rather than the one `closed-port` exercises in the stack's TCP handler.
#
# The OTHER branch of RFC 9293 section 3.10.7.1: the arriving segment carries
# an ACK, so the reset reuses it -- <SEQ=SEG.ACK><CTL=RST>, with NO ACK bit of
# its own and nothing to acknowledge. Both branches live in this file on
# purpose: written down separately, a later edit cannot quietly collapse them
# into one form without a vector failing.
0.000 < .  9000:9000(0) ack 700 win 65535
0.000 > R  700:700(0) win 0


# scenario: syn-retransmission
#
# A retransmitted SYN for an existing half-open connection re-sends the SYN-ACK
# rather than opening a second connection -- and with the SAME ISS. A peer that
# has already recorded the first sequence number either fails to connect or
# resets when a second, different one arrives.
#
# The vector is only worth writing if it can FAIL. A harness that injects a
# constant ISS cannot make it fail: a stack that regenerated the number on
# every SYN would draw the same constant and match these lines exactly. So the
# generator behind these numbers answers a DIFFERENT value on every call --
# 1000, then 1000000 higher, and so on. `1000` appearing twice below is
# therefore a statement that the number was REUSED from the block the first SYN
# created, not merely that some constant came back.
0.000 < S  0:0(0) win 65535
0.000 > S. 1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
# Two hundred milliseconds later, and nothing in between: a stack that
# retransmits its own SYN-ACK on a timer would be caught by the runner as an
# unaccounted frame.
0.200 < S  0:0(0) win 65535
0.200 > S. 1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>


# scenario: ecn-setup-syn
#
# A SYN with the ECN negotiation bits set (CWR and ECE, `W` and `E` here)
# behaves EXACTLY as a plain SYN. Linux sends this shape by default in several
# configurations, so it is not an exotic case.
#
# `TCPHeader.parse` carries all eight raw bits of the flags byte through,
# including the two `TCPFlags` deliberately does not name. Any dispatch that
# compared flags for EQUALITY -- `flags == [.syn]` -- would fail to see a SYN
# here and drop the connection attempt on the floor. Every dispatch in the
# state machine uses `.contains` for this reason, and this vector is what says
# so from the outside.
#
# We answer with a plain SYN-ACK: no ECE, because this stack has no ECN
# behaviour to give those bits and RFC 3168 section 6.1.1 has a non-ECN server
# answer an ECN-setup SYN exactly this way.
0.000 < SEW 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460,sackOK>
0.000 > S.  1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460,sackOK>
0.010 < .   1:1(0) ack 1001 win 512


# scenario: syn-with-payload
#
# A SYN carrying data. Legal, rare, and the sequence accounting is easy to get
# wrong in a way nothing else notices.
#
# We acknowledge the SYN ALONE -- `ack 1`, not `ack 5`. The four bytes are
# dropped rather than queued, and acknowledging bytes that were dropped would
# black-hole them: the guest would consider them delivered and never send them
# again. Acknowledging only what was actually taken leaves the peer to
# retransmit, which is what the last two lines show it doing.
0.000 < S  0:4(4) win 65535
0.000 > S. 1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .  1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
# The same four bytes again, now inside the established connection. They begin
# at sequence 1 because the SYN consumed sequence 0. THIS is where they are
# acknowledged, and the ack proves the earlier copy never advanced RCV.NXT: had
# the SYN's payload been taken, RCV.NXT would already be 5 and this segment
# would be a duplicate.
0.020 < P. 1:5(4) ack 1001 win 65535
# Four bytes is nowhere near 2*RMSS, so RFC 9293 3.8.6.3 holds the
# acknowledgement until the timer releases it. What this scenario pins is the
# ack NUMBER, not its timing: 5 rather than 9 is what says the SYN's payload was
# never taken.
0.520 > .  1001:1001(0) ack 5 win 65535


# scenario: different-iss
#
# A second SYN on the same four-tuple carrying a DIFFERENT initial sequence
# number is a fresh connection attempt, not a retransmission of the first, and
# it must not be adopted into the half-open connection already there.
#
# 9000 sits well inside the receive window, so this segment is ACCEPTABLE by
# RFC 9293 section 3.10.7.4's test -- it is not rejected by being out of range,
# which is what makes it the dangerous shape. RFC 5961 section 4 answers it
# with a challenge ACK: <SEQ=SND.NXT><ACK=RCV.NXT>. Both numbers below are
# assertions that nothing moved. `ack 1` is still IRS+1 from the FIRST SYN, not
# 9001; `1001` is still ISS+1 from the block that already exists, not a
# newly generated ISS. Resetting the connection here instead would let any
# off-path attacker who can guess a four-tuple kill it with one forged SYN.
0.000 < S  0:0(0) win 65535
0.000 > S. 1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.200 < S  9000:9000(0) win 65535
0.200 > .  1001:1001(0) ack 1 win 65535
